alt-syscall: give "complete" a 32-bit (compat) allowlist

complete_compat_whitelist[] was empty on purpose ("For now not adding a
32-bit-compatible version"), so under the enforcing "complete" table every
syscall a 32-bit process makes goes to block_syscall(). Measured on
amd64-openfyde_iris (.194) with a freestanding i686 probe after
prctl(PR_ALT_SYSCALL, PR_ALT_SYSCALL_SET_SYSCALL_TABLE, "complete"):
write, getpid, getuid32 and exit all logged "blocked syscall N" and the
process died with SIGSEGV when exit returned. complete_permissive logged
"compat syscall N not whitelisted" for the same calls.

This patch fills the list with the same policy as the 64-bit one: a 32-bit
syscall is allowed when its 64-bit counterpart is (same name, or its 32-bit
spelling: *_time64, *64, *32, _llseek, mmap2, stat64, sig* for rt_sig*,
the legacy path calls for their *at forms, socketcall/ipc for the calls
they multiplex, set/get_thread_area for arch_prctl). Everything the 64-bit
list blocks stays blocked. The list was generated from syscall_32.tbl
against the 64-bit entries and covers 388 of 452 i386 and 363 of 415 ARM
EABI syscalls; the denied remainder is the 64-bit denials (bpf, io_uring,
userfaultfd, the mount API, landlock, ...) plus 32-bit-only relics
(vm86, create_module, pciconfig_*, ...).

The numbers come from the kernel's generated headers (__NR_ia32_* on
x86_64, __NR_compat32_* on arm64), which cover the full 32-bit ABI; the
hand-written __NR_compat_* aliases stop at what Android needed. prctl keeps
alt_sys_prctl, as in the 64-bit list, so a 32-bit process cannot switch
tables either.

The entries mirror the 64-bit list *including* the rseq/clone3/faccessat2/
close_range/pidfd_open/openat2 additions of the follow-up patch, which is
present on every kernel that carries this one.
Index: v6.12-rpi/security/chromiumos/complete_whitelists.h
===================================================================
--- v6.12-rpi.orig/security/chromiumos/complete_whitelists.h
+++ v6.12-rpi/security/chromiumos/complete_whitelists.h
@@ -367,13 +367,449 @@
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_COMPAT
 /*
- * For now not adding a 32-bit-compatible version of the complete whitelist.
- * Since we are not whitelisting any compat syscalls here, a call into the
- * compat section of this "complete" alt syscall table will be redirected to
- * block_syscall() (unless the permissive mode is used in which case the call
- * will be redirected to warn_compat_syscall()).
+ * The 32-bit (compat) half of "complete": the same policy as the 64-bit list
+ * above, applied to the i386 ABI on x86_64 and the ARM EABI on arm64. A
+ * 32-bit syscall is allowed when its 64-bit counterpart is: the same name, or
+ * the 32-bit spelling of it (*_time64, *64, *32, _llseek, mmap2, stat64,
+ * the sig* calls for rt_sig*, the legacy path calls for their *at forms,
+ * socketcall and ipc for the socket and SysV calls they multiplex,
+ * set/get_thread_area for arch_prctl). Everything the 64-bit list blocks
+ * stays blocked: bpf, io_uring, userfaultfd, the new mount API, landlock,
+ * vm86 and the rest.
+ *
+ * Without this list every syscall a 32-bit process makes is sent to
+ * block_syscall(), so under the enforcing table a 32-bit program cannot even
+ * write() or exit().
+ *
+ * The numbers come from the headers the kernel generates from its own
+ * syscall_32.tbl (__NR_ia32_* on x86_64, __NR_compat32_* on arm64), which
+ * cover the whole 32-bit ABI; the hand-written __NR_compat_* aliases in
+ * alt-syscall.h and asm/unistd.h stop at the calls Android needed.
  */
-static const struct syscall_whitelist_entry complete_compat_whitelist[] __initconst = {};
+#if defined(CONFIG_X86_64)
+#include <asm/unistd_32_ia32.h>
+#define COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY_ALT(name, func)                           \
+        {                                                               \
+                .nr = __NR_ia32_ ## name,                               \
+                .alt = (sys_call_ptr_t)func,                            \
+        }
+#elif defined(CONFIG_ARM64)
+#include <asm/unistd_compat_32.h>
+#define COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY_ALT(name, func)                           \
+        {                                                               \
+                .nr = __NR_compat32_ ## name,                           \
+                .alt = (sys_call_ptr_t)func,                            \
+        }
+#endif
+#define COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY(name) COMPLETE_COMPAT_ENTRY_ALT(name, NULL)
